{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And the riffraff that was among them craved a craving.</b> All these words are connected, and this is their meaning: For the children of Israel knew the truth — that the fire of HaShem that burned against them was on the side of punishment, not by chance; therefore they ceased from their complaint. But the mixed multitude, whose origin was from the Egyptians steeped in licentiousness, did not take rebuke, and they continued in rebellion, bringing out from the mouth outward — everything they had first pondered in the inner chamber of their heart. And this is what it says: ״וְהָאַסַפְסֻף אֲשֶׁר״ — first ״בְּקִרְבּוֹ״, in the inner chamber of his heart — ״הִתְאַוּוּ תַּאֲוָה״, they craved. ״וַיָּשֻׁבוּ״ — that is, they returned to their rebellion, and brought their complaints outward and wept. ״וַיִּבְכּוּ גַּם בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — from the fact that it says ״גַַם״, infer from it that the mixed multitude wept first and caused the children of Israel to weep too. And the expression ״הִתְאַוּוּ תַּאֲוָה״ was doubled, for such is the way with forbidden sexual relations — desire drags desire, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Sukkah 52b): 'He satisfies him while still hungry.' ״וַיֹּאמְרוּ״ with a full mouth: ״מִי יַאֲכִלֵנוּ בָשָׂר״ — that is, flesh of forbidden sexual relations, and he used an elegant euphemism, as we say in the Gemara: 'We remember the fish' — he used an elegant euphemism. And all the more so, the expression 'flesh' may be interpreted this way." "And the fact that they began with 'flesh' and ended with 'fish' is because the term 'fish' conveys licentiousness in forbidden relations more than the term 'flesh,' since fish are more promiscuous than the flesh of any living creature. Therefore they said by way of compromise, ״מִי יַאֲכִלֵנוּ בָשָׂר״ — that we should be permitted in forbidden relations like the flesh of any living creature, even though we would not be like fish — for this much they did not ask. Nevertheless they said: ״זָכַרְנוּ אֶת הַדָּגָה אֲשֶׁר נֹאכַל בְּמִצְרַיִם חִנָּם״ — for when we were among the Egyptians steeped in licentiousness, everything was permitted to us, even to be like fish, and now let it be — and we shall be like the flesh of any living creature. And this is what it says: ״מִי יַאֲכִלֵנוּ בָשָׂר״.\n\nAnd it is plausible to hear it all quite literally, but their intent was to seek the flesh that heats the most and increases the desire for the act of mating, and this is the doubling of ״הִתְאַוּוּ תַּאֲוָה״ — that they craved the thing that increases desire for the act of mating. And fish, although they are cooling, nevertheless produce moisture and semen is included as well. Moreover, one who eats it acquires a nature to be promiscuous in forbidden relations. And there is in fish a twofold disadvantage; therefore they said by way of compromise, ״מִי יַאֲכִלֵנוּ בָשָׂר״ — that which sustains desire — and ״זָכַרְנוּ אֶת הַדָּגָה״ etc. — that which sustains promiscuity even more; nevertheless, let us have flesh by way of compromise.\n\nAnd according to this interpretation, what Moses said literally fits: ״מֵאַיִן לִי בָּשָׂר… אִם אֶת כָּל דְּגֵי הַיָּם״ etc., for they pressed their claim concerning these two things that aid fertility and procreation, and HaShem gave them the quail, which heats the most and fills their request. And so they said in the Midrash (Tanchuma 16): It is written (Psalms 78:27), ״וַיַּמְטֵר עֲלֵיהֶם כֶּעָפָר שְׁאֵר״, and it is written further on (Leviticus 18:6), ״אִישׁ אֶל כָּל שְׁאֵר בְּשָׂרוֹ״ — that is, forbidden relations — for this is the thing that their soul sought." "And if you say: in any case, fish are cooling and diminish desire — then explain the verses thus: They asked for flesh that heats and increases desire, and they said, 'We remember the fish etc. and the cucumbers and the melons' — for all these things are cooling, and nevertheless they would not harm procreation, because we also had the leeks and the onions and the garlic, which heat the most and negate the coldness; but now, ״נַפְשֵׁנוּ יְבֵשָׁה״ etc." "Or say that in Egypt those cooling things did not harm us. Also the leeks and the garlic and the onions, although they are hard for pregnant and nursing women, nevertheless we were fruitful and multiplied, and all the things that are contrary to procreation — whether for the men or for the women — did not harm us at all. And now, ״נַפְשֵׁנוּ יְבֵשָׁה״, for we do not have so much moisture that multiplies semen, for ״בִּלְתִּי אֶל הַמָּן לְבַד עֵינֵינוּ״, and it is absorbed in the limbs and is not digested in the digestive tract and in the liver, and therefore does not cause procreation.\n\nBut the verse testifies to the opposite and says ״וְהַמָּן כִּזְרַע״ — that its appearance was like seed, to tell you that it multiplies semen. And it was גַּד, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Yoma 75a), that it would indicate whether the firstborn was conceived in the ninth month or the second in the seventh — corresponding to the numerical value of גַּד, which is what it would indicate when one was a seventh-month child. And all this pertains to things that aid procreation.\n\nThey said that the manna caused dryness because it has no moisture, but Scripture says: ״וְטַעְמוֹ כְּטַעַם לְשַׁד הַשָּׁמֶן״ — that is, moisture — and this is the opposite of dryness." "And regarding what they said, ״אֵין כֹּל״ — some say they meant: although it has the taste of every kind of food, nevertheless ״אֵין כֹּל״ — it does not have the taste of all foods, for it does not have the taste of leeks and onions etc.\n\nAnd in the Akeidah, he explained ״בִּלְתִּי אֶל הַמָּן עֵינֵינוּ״ thus: although it has all the tastes, nevertheless our eyes see nothing but the manna, and the blind man eats and is not satisfied."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {4 "The Akeidah refers to R. Isaac Arama's philosophical work; ״הַסּוּמָא אוֹכֵל וְאֵינוֹ שָׂבֵעַ״ is likely an idiom for unsatisfying eating rather than a literal blind person."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:03.887567033Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}